b F_p$ (an…","reading_title":{"format":"inline-parts-v1","source":"Reduction is injective on prime-to-$p$ torsion","parts":[["text","Reduction is injective on prime-to-"],["math","\\(p\\)"],["text"," torsion"]]},"route":"../roadmap/model/descent/index.html#reduction-torsion"},{"id":"model/descent/reduction-point-counts","title":"Point counts at $(17,s-6)$ and $(23,s-5)$","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Lemma","lean":"ZInQ.pointCounts_17_23","summary":"The following closed statements hold:","reading_title":{"format":"inline-parts-v1","source":"Point counts at $(17,s-6)$ and $(23,s-5)$","parts":[["text","Point counts at "],["math","\\((17,s-6)\\)"],["text"," and "],["math","\\((23,s-5)\\)"]]},"route":"../roadmap/model/descent/index.html#reduction-point-counts"},{"id":"model/descent/torsion","title":"The torsion of $E(K_0)$ is the full two-torsion","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.mem_torsion_E_iff, ZInQ.card_torsion_E, ZInQ.genP0_not_isOfFinAddOrder, ZInQ.mem_torsion_C_iff, ZInQ.card_torsion_C","summary":"Every point of finite order in $E(K_0)$ lies in $E(K_0)[2]=\\{O,(0,0),(d,0),(-d,0)\\}$. Thus the torsion subgroup of $E(K_0)$ is $E(K_0)[2]$, of order $4$ and isomorphic to $(\\mathbb Z/2\\mathbb Z)^2$. The same holds for $C(K_0)$, whose torsion subgroup is $C(K_0)[2]=\\{O,(0,0),(\\delta,0),(-\\delta,0)\\}$.","reading_title":{"format":"inline-parts-v1","source":"The torsion of $E(K_0)$ is the full two-torsion","parts":[["text","The torsion of "],["math","\\(E(K_0)\\)"],["text"," is the full two-torsion"]]},"route":"../roadmap/model/descent/index.html#torsion"},{"id":"model/descent/nonzero-multiples","title":"Nonzero multiples of the generator","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Lemma","lean":"ZInQ.genP0_not_isOfFinAddOrder, ZInQ.iota_injective, ZInQ.zsmul_genP0_affine, ZInQ.zsmul_genP0_affine_z_w, ZInQ.iota_zero_coord_iff","summary":"The point $P_0$ has infinite order. Consequently, for every nonzero $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ the point $[m]P_0$ is affine, its abscissa and ordinate are nonzero, and $z([m]P_0)=-x/y$ and $w([m]P_0)=1/x$ are nonzero elements of $K_0$. The map $m\\mapsto\\iota(m)$ is injective, and the first coordinate of $\\iota(m)$ is $0$ exactly when $m\\ne0$.","route":"../roadmap/model/descent/index.html#nonzero-multiples"},{"id":"model/descent/rank-one","title":"$E(K_0)$ has rank one","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.exists_generator_E, ZInQ.exists_generator_C","summary":"Assume that $E(K_0)$ is finitely generated (the Mordell-Weil hypothesis). Then there is a point $S\\in C(K_0)$ of infinite order such that every $P\\in C(K_0)$ is uniquely $P=[m]S+T$ with $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $T\\in C(K_0)[2]$: The same holds for $E(K_0)$ with $\\theta^{-1}(S)$ and $E(K_0)[2]$.","reading_title":{"format":"inline-parts-v1","source":"$E(K_0)$ has rank one","parts":[["math","\\(E(K_0)\\)"],["text"," has rank one"]]},"route":"../roadmap/model/descent/index.html#rank-one"},{"id":"model/descent/odd-free-index","title":"The free index of $R$ is odd","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Lemma","lean":"ZInQ.odd_free_index","summary":"Let $S\\in C(K_0)$ be as in the rank-one theorem and write $R=(1+s,2s)=[m]S+T$ with $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $T\\in C(K_0)[2]$. Then $m$ is odd.","reading_title":{"format":"inline-parts-v1","source":"The free index of $R$ is odd","parts":[["text","The free index of "],["math","\\(R\\)"],["text"," is odd"]]},"route":"../roadmap/model/descent/index.html#odd-free-index"},{"id":"model/descent/mordell-weil-from-descent","title":"Mordell-Weil for $E/K_0$ from the descent","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.mordellWeilE","summary":"The group $E(K_0)$ is finitely generated; that is, the Mordell-Weil hypothesis for the generator curve holds.","reading_title":{"format":"inline-parts-v1","source":"Mordell-Weil for $E/K_0$ from the descent","parts":[["text","Mordell-Weil for "],["math","\\(E/K_0\\)"],["text"," from the descent"]]},"route":"../roadmap/model/d